 point of view you  yourself have been pretty critical of  the government the um  of uh the johnson  for  administration  underpinning its legal measures with  behavioral  uh science and behavioral nudges um  are you supportive of what they've done  this week on travel restrictions  i think the example of travel  restrictions is an example where we had  a dysfunctional system  dysfunctional because of the  all the mess around getting  pcr tests all the different companies  charging absurd rates not providing a  service and so on and they've  responded not by improving the system  but by abandoning it entirely and i  think that's a pity i do think we need  pcr tests  because everybody always said that  lateral flow tests whenever what we call  green light tests test to say you're  safe and therefore we can uh you you can  act as if you don't have the virus what  they do is they pick up asymptomatic  cases and very very valuable in that  regard i think it would be far  preferable to keep pcr tests but to  improve the system and and to do them  through the nhs so i think it is  increasing risk i think it does limit  in fact it stops our ability to trace  different variants and increases the  probability of of infected people coming  into the country so i think it has  increased the risk quite frankly and i  think we should have improved the system  rather than by and large abandoning it  okay so  so you're against the dropping of the  pcr test do you think  that the  decision to offer booster jabs to  everyone uh is actually a good  step forward well rather than doing this  piecemeal let me give you my sense of  the overall direction of travel we  should have because  if you remember back to last year 21st  of september last year sage made a  recommendation that we need to put  protections  in place in order to  avoid the pandemic getting out of  control and the government ignored that  they ignored it for nearly two months  the pandemic got so far out of control  that